# The red branch is reversed, and a sentence above it hides that

Round 2 of #1905 (B3). The check there took the first occurrence of
`--remove-label review:approved` in the whole file and compared it against the
first `--add-label review:changes-requested`. Reversing the red branch alone was
caught. Reversing it and mentioning the flag in prose first was not.

That mention is not adversarial. `memory/workflow/review/memory.md` already
makes the same claim in prose, so restating it with the flag spelled out is an
ordinary next edit. The file then says the right thing in prose and hands over a
copy-pasteable block that does the wrong thing — #1884's window, reopened.

The mention sits INSIDE the red segment here, not above it, which is what makes
this case load bearing twice over. A guard that scopes by segment but counts any
flag as an instruction reads the prose mention as the removal and goes green; so
does a guard that compares first occurrences across the file. Only counting a
flag that a `gh pr edit` candidate actually carries separates the two.
